# Sibyl configuration.
# Copy this file to `.env` and edit. `.env` is gitignored.
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# Every value below has a working default baked into sibyl.py. Nothing here is
# required to run: an empty .env, or no .env at all, works fine.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HOTKEY
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# One key on its own, or several held together with a '+'.
#
# A gesture fires only if the exact set of keys went down and back up within
# TAP_MAX_HOLD seconds with no other key in between. That is what lets real
# modifiers double as a hotkey: Ctrl+C stays Ctrl+C, and Ctrl+Alt+anything
# sends nothing. Finger order does not matter.
#
# Use pynput key names: alt_r, ctrl_r, alt_l, ctrl_l, f9, f10, scroll_lock,
# pause, insert. Run `.\Start-Sibyl.cmd hotkeys` to see what your keyboard
# actually reports before committing to one. It prints every key with the
# numeric code Windows sent, which is what Sibyl matches on.
#
# Why two keys: tapping Alt on its own is how Windows opens a program's menu
# bar, so a lone Right Alt would fight the operating system every time.
#
# Names are matched by their numeric code, so alt_r and alt_gr are the same
# key here and either spelling works.
#
# On UK, German, French and other non-US layouts, Right Alt is AltGr and types
# special characters. Pick a different key there.

# HOTKEY=ctrl_r+alt_r

# Hold the keys to talk and let go to send. Tap them quickly instead and Sibyl
# keeps listening (hands-free) until the next tap. This is the boundary between
# the two, in seconds. Raise it if your quick taps are being read as holds;
# lower it if a short hold keeps turning into hands-free.
# TAP_MAX_HOLD=0.5

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OLLAMA
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Sibyl's own setting, not Ollama's OLLAMA_HOST, which Sibyl ignores. Only this
# computer is accepted, written as http://127.0.0.1:<port>; anything else is
# refused at start, and your words are pasted without the clean-up.
# SIBYL_OLLAMA_HOST=http://127.0.0.1:11434

# The one model, which cleans up what you said. Must be pulled first:
#   ollama pull qwen2.5:3b
# Chosen by measurement: see the comment on CLEANUP_MODEL in sibyl.py, and
# run `python eval_cleanup.py --model <name> --runs 3` before switching.
# CLEANUP_MODEL=qwen2.5:3b

# Ceiling on how much the model may write, so a runaway cannot turn a
# few-second wait into a minute. Normal output is far below it. Raising it
# widens the model's window to match, and so costs a little more memory.
# MAX_TOKENS_CLEANUP=512

# Two-tier timeout, in seconds. The first call of a session blocks while Ollama
# loads the model into memory, so it gets longer.
# OLLAMA_TIMEOUT_COLD=180
# OLLAMA_TIMEOUT_WARM=120

# How long Sibyl waits at startup for Ollama to answer before showing the notch
# red, in seconds. Ollama normally starts with Windows, so this only matters on
# a machine where it starts late or not at all.
# OLLAMA_WAIT_AT_START=60

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SPEECH TO TEXT
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Backend is pluggable, so trying another speech engine is a config change
# rather than a rewrite.
# STT_BACKEND=faster-whisper

# tiny.en < base.en < small.en < medium.en, trading speed for accuracy.
# base.en is the starting point; measure with --test-stt before changing.
# STT_MODEL=base.en

# int8 is roughly 2-4x faster than float32 on CPU for a small accuracy cost.
# STT_COMPUTE_TYPE=int8

# cpu, or cuda on a machine with an NVIDIA graphics card. On a machine without
# one, cuda raises an error rather than running slowly.
# STT_DEVICE=cpu

# Names the recogniser is told to expect. It cannot guess a spelling it has
# never heard, so put your own names, places and jargon here, separated by
# commas. Measured 2026-09-20: this more than halved the word errors and cost
# 0.03s. Set it to nothing to switch the hint off.
# STT_HOTWORDS=Sibyl, Qwen, Ollama, Champs-Élysées, Siobhan, Joaquin.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# RECORDING
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Hard cap on a single capture, in seconds. Protects you from walking away
# with the recorder still running. Raising it widens the model's window to
# match, so a longer dictation still fits: 120 seconds gives a window of
# 3,072 word pieces, 300 seconds gives 4,096. That costs a little more memory.
# MAX_RECORD_SECONDS=120
